// TEST: AD Gamma Log-Likelihood Shape (lgamma derivative)
import ad as ad
// ============================================================================
// The positive half of the derivRule change that 014 used to reject:
// d/dx lgamma(x) = digamma(x). This is the rule that unlocks HMC over the
// gamma / poisson / beta families, whose log-densities are all "closed form
// plus an lgamma normalizer" -- with lgamma refused, their gradients were not
// expressible at all.
//
// f has the shape of a Gamma(a, rate) log-density read as a function of the
// SHAPE parameter, with the rate and the observation frozen as literals:
//
//   f(a) = a*log(r0) - lgamma(a) + (a - 1)*log(x0)      r0 = 2.5, x0 = 1.75
//   f'(a) = log(r0) - digamma(a) + log(x0)
//
// so the pinned gradient exercises the new rule in the one position that
// matters -- lgamma sitting under a minus sign inside a larger expression,
// where a silent zero-derivative (the pre-split behaviour) would have gone
// unnoticed as a plausible-looking number rather than a NaN.
//
// The gradient value is independently checkable: digamma(3.5) = psi(1/2) +
// 1/0.5 + 1/1.5 + 1/2.5 with psi(1/2) = -gamma - 2*log 2, giving
// 1.1031566406452431, and log(2.5) + log(1.75) - that = 0.37274987916433466.
// A central finite difference of f at a = 3.5 converges to the same value.
//
// Both evaluators must agree: the compiled path calls blade_rt::digamma and
// the interpreter calls its transcription, which is exactly the byte-identity
// claim pinned in intrinsics/010.
// ============================================================================
function f(a: Float) -> Float = a * log(2.5) - lgamma(a) + (a - 1.0) * log(1.75)
let (fv, dfda) = ad.grad(f)(3.5)
// EXPECT: fv = 3.405083429051024
// EXPECT: dfda = 0.37274987916433466
